{
  "context": [
    "ASCII uses 7 bits to represent 128 characters, with codes from 0 through 127. The figure shows each code as an 8-bit byte in groups of four bits. The highest bit is always 0. The table below combines the figure's four panels into one complete table in ascending decimal order."
  ],
  "description": [
    "To look up a range: codes 0 through 31 and 127 are control characters. DEL at 127 means delete (unlabeled in the original figure). Code 32 is space (SP); 48 through 57 are the digits 0 through 9; 65 through 90 are uppercase A through Z; and 97 through 122 are lowercase a through z. Printable punctuation and symbols occupy 33–47, 58–64, 91–96, and 123–126.",
    "The English figure labels LF (10) “Line feed,” FF (12) “Form feed,” and CR (13) “Carriage return.” The original Simplified Chinese figure uses the word “key” for these functions, which do not require a physical keypress. SO (14), labeled “Shift out,” selects an alternate character set. SI (15), labeled “Shift in,” switches back. ESC (27), labeled “Escape,” introduces an escape sequence. The word “overflow” in the original Chinese label for ESC does not indicate arithmetic overflow.",
    "A character literal differs from its numeric code. The digit character 0 has code 48 (binary 0011 0000), whereas code 0 is the null character NUL (binary 0000 0000). Both differ from space, SP (32), and delete, DEL (127)."
  ],
  "frames": [
    {
      "book_path": "chapter_data_structure/character_encoding.assets/ascii_table.png",
      "explanation": "The digit characters 0 through 9 all have 0011 as their high four bits, with the low four bits running from 0000 through 1001. The uppercase and lowercase forms of a letter differ by 32, in only the sixth bit from the right (weight 32). For example, A is 65 (0100 0001) and a is 97 (0110 0001). To look up line feed, find LF in the row for decimal 10 below.",
      "id": "source-43",
      "title": "ASCII characters and codes"
    }
  ],
